
A progressive wave transfers energy without transferring material and is 
made up of particles of a medium/field oscillating. 



Amplitude - A wave's maximum displacement from the equilibrium position. Measured in metres 

Frequency - The number of complete oscillations passing through a point per second. Measured in Hertz(Hz). 

Wavelength - The length of one whole oscillation. Measured in metres 

Speed - Distance travelled by the wave per unit time. 

Period - Time taken for one complete oscillation. 

Phase - The  position of a certain point on a wave cycle. 

Phase Difference - How much a particle/wave lags behind another particle/wave. Measured in radians.


C = f  x λ  (wavespeed equation) 

f = 1 / T   (period equation)

Transverse Waves - oscillations of particles/fields at right angles to the direction
                   of energy transfer. 

.All waves part of the EM spectrum are transverse. 

.All EM waves travel at 3 x 10^8 ms^-1 (speed of light) in a vacuum.


Longitudinal waves - Oscillations of particles that are parallel to the direction
                     of energy transfer. 

.They are comprises of regions of compression and rarefactions and can't travel in 
  a vacuum. 

.Sound and ultrasound are examples of longitudinal waves. 


